WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.440
You’ve probably seen the headlines.
Uber burnt through its AI budget in 4 months.&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.720
Another company spent $500 million in just one.&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.200
Token costs have even gone “far&nbsp;
beyond the costs of the employees”.&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.320
Yet, all these companies tried to&nbsp;
spend as much as possible on tokens.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.760
Companies have wildly overspent on&nbsp;
tokens, and are now cutting back.&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.680
But, all of this is especially weird, because&nbsp;
tokens aren’t actually getting more expensive.&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.440
They’re getting cheaper.&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.640
The price of a single token has&nbsp;
fallen by about 90% since 2023.&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.840
But, AI spend keeps going up.
So what’s going on?&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.840
Why did cheaper tokens make&nbsp;
AI more expensive than ever?&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:54.480
Something’s happened that may have&nbsp;
changed the AI industry for good.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.760
It feels like just a few months ago,&nbsp;
companies were pushing every employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.760
to spend as many tokens as possible.
Many were calling it… “tokenmaxxing”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.160
literally meaning 'maximizing token usage’.
You can probably see where this is going…

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.480
At Uber, engineers were given&nbsp;
access to Claude Code and Cursor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:28.320
and by March 2026, 84% of Uber's developers&nbsp;
were classified as agentic coding users.&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.840
Then, Uber placed a cap on employee AI spend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.103
after going through their entire&nbsp;
AI budget in just 4 months.&nbsp;

00:01:36.103 --> 00:01:37.280
[Praveen Neppalli Naga, Chief Technology&nbsp;
Officer, Uber]: "I'm back to the drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.840
board because the budget I thought&nbsp;
I would need is blown away already”.&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.440
Even worse, Uber’s COO and president said
[Andrew Macdonald, Uber President/COO] “If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.320
you’re not actually able to draw a direct line&nbsp;
to how [many] useful features and functionality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.880
you’re shipping to your users, that trade becomes&nbsp;
harder to justify. That link is not there yet.”&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.240
But, this list goes on and on.
In 30 days, Meta workers reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.960
ran through 60 trillion Claude tokens.
Microsoft encouraged employees to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.680
different AI tools, but is now pushing&nbsp;
everyone back to GitHub Copilot.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.040
Even though this is framed as&nbsp;
“consolidation”, most see it as cost cutting.&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.080
Amazon set targets for more than 80%&nbsp;
of developers to use AI each week.&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.600
They even had an AI leaderboard, KiroRank,&nbsp;
which tracked AI token use among employees.&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.960
Employees then began inflating their scores&nbsp;
through “tokenmaxxing”. Basically giving AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.320
useless tasks to increase token use.
KiroRank was then taken offline.&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:45.520
Amazon’s Senior Vice President said “Please&nbsp;
don’t use AI just for the sake of using AI,”.&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.160
An even stranger case is NVIDIA.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.336
About a month later, an NVIDIA executive said
[Bryan Catanzaro, vice president of applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.336 --> 00:03:34.640
deep learning, NVIDIA]: “For my team, the cost of&nbsp;
compute is far beyond the costs of the employees”.&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.320
Another anonymous AI client burned through $500&nbsp;
million on AI in just one month, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.120
didn’t set limits on employee licenses.
Shopify, Spotify, ServiceNow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.000
and Roku all mentioned in their earnings&nbsp;
calls that AI has become a major pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.400
point on operating expenses.
Not only is the cost spiking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.800
but so are the problems.
One being “code churn”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:05.280
Aka lines of code deleted versus added.
Alex Circei, the CEO of Waydev, said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:12.400
managers are seeing code acceptance rates of 80%&nbsp;
to 90%, aka the AI code developers approve and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.560
keep, but don’t consider the amount of revision&nbsp;
engineers have to do in the following weeks.&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.240
“Which drives the real-world acceptance rate&nbsp;
down between 10% and 30% of generated code.”&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.040
GitClear found in a report that AI tools&nbsp;
did make developers more productive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.210
but the amount of code churn they had to do was&nbsp;
2.2x greater than their productivity increase.&nbsp;

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:42.320
Faros AI found that, based on two years of&nbsp;
customer data, code churn had increased by 861%.&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.880
Another study of 2,444 companies found that&nbsp;
for every dollar a company spends on AI tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:55.440
$0.44 is used to fix bugs generated by AI,&nbsp;
$0.27 to rewrite AI-produced code, and $0.11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.160
is consumed in review and merge delays.
“In other words, behind every dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.800
of AI procurement cost lies&nbsp;
nearly 80% in hidden losses.”&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.440
People are calling this the “Tokenpocalypse”.
Companies tied token consumption to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.640
productivity and output, but all&nbsp;
that happened was way higher costs.&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.400
They all fell into the same&nbsp;
problem: Goodhart's Law.&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.280
When a measure becomes a target,&nbsp;
it ceases to be a good measure.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.280
Silicon Valley, collectively, just&nbsp;
went through a very strange experiment.&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.360
Goldman Sachs reported that companies&nbsp;
are “overrunning their initial budgets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.480
for inference by orders of magnitude.”
And what makes all this even stranger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.520
is that token cost, has gone down.
90% since 2023.&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:48.080
By other metrics, they’ve dropped by 280x.
But simultaneously AI spend is exploding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.600
by an estimated 320%.
Worldwide IT spending is expected to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:06:02.000
$6.31 trillion in 2026, up 13.5% year over year.
More than the entire annual GDP of the UK.&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.000
U.S. enterprises have become more&nbsp;
cost-conscious in their AI spending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.760
and this has caused another surprising&nbsp;
phenomenon, more on that later.&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.120
More importantly, why isn’t more token&nbsp;
usage converting into better results?&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:30.080
Why does it feel like AI&nbsp;
is getting more expensive?

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.480
Before we can answer this, what&nbsp;
does “token” actually mean?&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.880
LLMs like ChatGPT don’t understand language. They&nbsp;
can’t read what you say, see an image you send,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.480
or hear the audio you speak.
“The word darkness, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.440
would be split into two tokens, “dark”&nbsp;
and “ness,” with each token bearing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:55.920
numerical representation, such as 217 and&nbsp;
655. The model processes these input tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.640
generates its response as tokens and then&nbsp;
translates it to the user’s expected format.”&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.680
According to NVIDIA: “The goal is to achieve&nbsp;
the fastest processing time and lowest cost per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.920
token to optimize AI infrastructure&nbsp;
and maximize revenue generation”.&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.680
Keep that in mind, because something that’s&nbsp;
happening now, makes that claim rather ironic.&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.960
And as a disclaimer I’m not&nbsp;
an AI expert, not even close.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.760
But what I am extremely interested in is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.240
business side, and why many businesses&nbsp;
have done a 180 on their token spend.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:32.240
There are a few factors to this.&nbsp;
The first is that tokens consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.280
don’t scale linearly with productive output.
A study by Jellyfish found that larger token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.800
budgets did result in more pull requests,&nbsp;
aka, proposed changes to a shared codebase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.680
but ten times the tokens,&nbsp;
produced about 2 times the output.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.800
“Tokens, in this sense, behave less like&nbsp;
a linear input and more like rocket fuel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.400
Going faster is possible, but it requires&nbsp;
exponentially more resources to do so.”&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.760
Another problem, one that’s&nbsp;
perhaps most surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.400
comes from the more advanced reasoning models.
Remember what NVIDIA said about “fastest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.400
processing time”? Well, that’s&nbsp;
where we run into a problem.&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.680
Claude Opus, GPT o3, the ones that can&nbsp;
tackle harder problems, spend minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.960
or even hours thinking to solve a problem.
“Reasoning AI models, the latest advancement in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:26.960
LLMs, can tackle more complex queries by treating&nbsp;
tokens differently than before. Here, in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.880
to input and output tokens, the model generates&nbsp;
a host of reasoning tokens over minutes or hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:39.280
as it thinks about how to solve a given problem.”
The power of these things, is pretty unbelievable.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.440
So powerful, that the US Government has&nbsp;
issued an export control on Claude Fable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.360
due to national security risks.
But, the more powerful models have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.760
caused an… interesting problem to occur.
If you ask your friend “What’s 1+1”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.800
they’ll probably say “2” straight away.
But, imagine if they stopped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.200
and really deeply thought about it&nbsp;
for 30 seconds, and then said “2”.&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.680
You’d think “You already know the answer,&nbsp;
why’d you think so hard about it”.&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.240
That’s basically what’s happening.
AI right now has a bit of a reasoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:16.480
problem. Or, rather, an inefficiency problem.
If you ask some, not all, but some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.240
state-of-the-art reasoning LLMs&nbsp;
this question, they will stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.040
break down the question, deliberate over&nbsp;
it, and may even take 10 or 20 seconds.&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:29.360
They can break tasks into smaller&nbsp;
subtasks of a larger problem. Greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.960
potential output, but more tokens consumed.
What’s weird, is the “lighter” AI models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:40.160
can do this immediately, not that they inherently&nbsp;
do math, but based on training and probability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.200
the right response to your query is “2”.
[Firat Elbey, Principal Product Manager]:“Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.480
unnecessary reasoning cycle increases latency,&nbsp;
compounds infrastructure costs, and consumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.880
energy. Recent analyses suggest that unnecessary&nbsp;
prompt verbosity alone costs tens of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.680
dollars in excess computation annually”.
So even though all those engineers get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:05.120
encouraged to use more AI, if the way they’re&nbsp;
used isn’t extremely specific and intentional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.480
to the task, it can be totally off.
Complex task to a low-level model?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:15.360
More problems, inadequate results.
Easy task to a high-level model? Longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.280
task duration, overconsumption&nbsp;
of tokens, inefficient results.&nbsp;

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.360
[Firat Elbey, Principal Product Manager]:&nbsp;
”While these are powerful tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.720
they're often deployed indiscriminately across&nbsp;
a wide variety of tasks, including countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:33.120
queries that likely require no reasoning at all&nbsp;
— and this inefficiency has real consequences.”&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.760
All of this compounds even more,&nbsp;
when we look at Agentic AI.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:42.400
This is when you give an AI model a goal,&nbsp;
and a collection of tools. Running code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:47.200
searching the web, writing files,&nbsp;
calling APIs, and it runs autonomously.&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.000
But, while chatting is a single call and response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.600
an agent goes in a loop&nbsp;
until it deems the task done.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:57.920
Look at the result, think again,&nbsp;
do the next thing, repeat. Lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:03.680
of small steps, lots of tokens used.
But, this gets more expensive because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.480
every time it loops back to “think”,&nbsp;
it re-reads all the context so far.&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.680
This is the Agentic Loop Multiplier, and&nbsp;
it’s what’s driving up a lot of cost.&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:19.920
Goldman Sachs forecasts agentic AI could drive&nbsp;
a 24-fold increase in token consumption by 2030,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.840
reaching 120 quadrillion tokens per month.
With this in mind, you can see how easy it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:31.040
be for employees to waste tokens on… nothing.
Especially when they’re given a blanket rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.600
to “spend more tokens!”
Again: Goodhart's Law.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:41.360
More tokens, doesn’t always mean better results.
But, something doesn’t quite add up.&nbsp;

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:46.400
We still come back to the question: Why&nbsp;
did cheaper tokens cause a spike in cost?&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:51.600
Well, this isn’t actually anything new.
Not only that, have you noticed how almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.880
all AI companies have recently&nbsp;
changed their business model?&nbsp;

00:11:54.880 --> 00:12:08.320
We might be moving into a totally&nbsp;
new stage in the AI lifecycle.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:10.080
If you haven’t subscribed yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:16.560
consider subscribing. We’re on the road to&nbsp;
1 million and are over 90% there! Thank you.&nbsp;

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:20.400
Ironically, cheaper tokens,&nbsp;
are what drove more cost.&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.960
As cost of production decreases,&nbsp;
demand and consumption often increase.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.360
The Jevons paradox.
The more efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:30.480
you make a resource the greater the demand for it.&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:34.560
This gives us crucial context for&nbsp;
a major change in the world of AI.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:39.360
Have you noticed that many of these platforms have&nbsp;
changed from subscriptions to token use pricing?&nbsp;

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.200
There’s still subscriptions, of course,&nbsp;
but these just give you an allocation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.280
of tokens, which you can use daily.
The high spending engineers and companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:51.360
will likely be setting token budgets,&nbsp;
rather than a fixed subscription rate.&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.120
We’re experiencing what I think is a major market&nbsp;
transition we’ve seen in all kinds of industries.&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.080
Streaming services appear,&nbsp;
charging a low subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.880
gaining market adoption, then raising&nbsp;
prices and introducing new revenue streams.&nbsp;

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.720
Delivery apps did the same thing,&nbsp;
losing money to get market penetration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.400
then changing to focus on profit.
AI is doing the same thing, except changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:20.320
the model to charge based on token use.
Since spring 2025, every major AI agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:25.920
company — Cursor, Vercel's V0, Replit,&nbsp;
Lovable — almost simultaneously moved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:31.732
token-based billing, shrank or eliminated free&nbsp;
tiers, and pushed overage charges onto users.&nbsp;

00:13:31.732 --> 00:13:36.320
[Christian Klein, CEO, SAP] "It would be foolish&nbsp;
to still charge a subscription base, because AI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:42.320
so powerful that it will automate a lot of tasks”.
It’s not just AI models either.&nbsp;

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:48.400
Just a few years ago, most software as a service&nbsp;
companies used to use seat-based pricing.&nbsp;

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:53.680
But with AI agents, that’s largely disappearing.
Hence why so many are overhauling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:58.080
their entire business model.
We’re at a tipping point with AI right now.&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:02.240
When reviewing spending data from&nbsp;
over 50,000 US companies, Ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:07.680
a U.S. corporate spending management platform,&nbsp;
found DeepSeek, the Chinese AI, is rising fast.&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:12.000
“Past regulars on this list have been&nbsp;
Silicon Valley favorites like Figma and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.040
Fireworks AI—this is the first time a&nbsp;
Chinese model company has topped it.”&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:23.600
DeepSeek’s V4-Pro model will cost $3.48 for&nbsp;
1 million tokens of output; by comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:28.880
OpenAI and Anthropic charge $30 and $25&nbsp;
respectively for the same amount of work.&nbsp;

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.880
Another Chinese AI Kimi&nbsp;
cost $4 per million tokens.&nbsp;

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:37.040
Note that this is just price,&nbsp;
not quality of model output.&nbsp;

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:40.640
But not only are token prices&nbsp;
from Chinese AI cheaper, but their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:46.720
models are surpassing US ones in downloads.
Does this mean total use? I don’t think so.&nbsp;

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:53.120
Anthropic and OpenAI are still leading&nbsp;
corporate adoption at 34.4% and 32.3%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:57.280
but DeepSeek is securing a place&nbsp;
as the cost-effective alternative.&nbsp;

00:14:57.280 --> 00:15:02.640
I do think we’re moving from one stage of&nbsp;
AI to another, even if it takes a while.&nbsp;

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:07.600
Companies are becoming more critical of their&nbsp;
AI spend, and how it translates into output.&nbsp;

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:12.960
In a lot of cases, it’s just the&nbsp;
same results, but with more cost.&nbsp;

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.760
Once again, humans are underrated.
[Firat Elbey, Principal Product Manager]:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:18.640
“Efficient AI can learn from&nbsp;
human cognition's adaptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:22.240
resource allocation — knowing&nbsp;
when to engage deep processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.520
not just how to process deeply.”
[Whizy Kim, Techbrew]: “unlike AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:30.080
human workers can actually be held&nbsp;
accountable for their mistakes”&nbsp;

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:35.040
What I find particularly funny, is actually&nbsp;
Microsoft, pivoting back to Copilot.&nbsp;

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.640
They’ve had nothing but problems with&nbsp;
this thing, and managed to annoy seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:51.040
all 365 customers… and Windows users.
